ny line covering (2,1) and (2,2)? That's vertical line x=2, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line covering (2,1) and (3,1)? That's horizontal line y=1, non-sunny. So not allowed. So any line covering two of the remaining points is non-sunny. So each sunny line can cover at most one of the remaining points. So with two sunny lines, we can cover at most two of the three remaining points. So we cannot cover all three points. So k=2 impossible for n=3. So the solution's claim that k=2 impossible holds for n=3 as well.\n\nNow k=3: all three lines sunny. We need to cover 6 points with 3 sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. There are 3 columns (x=1,2,3). So each line can have at most 3 points (one per column). So total capacity = 3*3 = 9 > 6. So maybe possible. Let's try to find a construction.\n\nWe need to assign points to 3 lines such that each line is straight and sunny. Let's try to find lines.\n\nPoints:\n\n- Column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\n- Column 2: (2,1),(2,2)\n- Column 3: (3,1)\n\nWe need 3 lines. Let's try to assign:\n\nLine L1: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3?) but (3,3) not in set. So not possible.\n\nLine L1: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). Check if these are collinear: slope between (1,3) and (2,2) = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1/1 = -1, which is slope -1, non-sunny. So not allowed.\n\nLine L1: (1,2),(2,1),(3,0?) not in set.\n\nLine L1: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3?) not in set.\n\nLine L1: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1) are collinear horizontal, non-sunny.\n\nLine L1: (1,2),(2,2),(3,2?) not in set.\n\nLine L1: (1,3),(2,3?) not in set.\n\nThus any line that passes through two points from different columns may be vertical/horizontal/slope -1? Let's check all pairs:\n\n- (1,1)-(2,1): horizontal, non-sunny.\n- (1,1)-(2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, sunny. So line through (1,1) and (2,2) has slope 1, equation y = x. Does it pass through (3,3)? Not in set. So line L1: (1,1),(2,2) is sunny. It also passes through (3,3) which is not needed. So L1 covers (1,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\n- (1,1