ft (vertical or slope -1). We also have 2 sunny lines. Let's see if we can cover the rest of the points.\n\nThe rest of the points include columns 1,...,n-3, n-1, n. Column n-1 has points (n-1,1),(n-1,2). Column n has point (n,1). Column n-3 has points (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-3,4). Actually column n-3 has n+1-(n-3) = 4 points. So we need to cover many points.\n\nWe have n-5 non-sunny lines left. If we use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-5 (or some selection), we might be able to cover many points. But we need to check if we can cover column n-1 and n with only 2 sunny lines. As before, column n-1 has two points, column n has one point. The same issue arises: any line covering (n,1) and a point in column n-1 is either horizontal (if (n-1,1)) or slope -1 (if (n-1,2)). So a sunny line cannot cover (n,1) together with any point in column n-1. So (n,1) must be covered by a sunny line that does not also cover any point in column n-1. So that sunny line can only cover (n,1) and maybe points in earlier columns. Similarly, the other sunny line must cover the two points in column n-1, but any line covering both (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) is vertical (non-sunny). So we cannot cover both points in column n-1 with a single sunny line. So we need at least two sunny lines to cover the two points in column n-1, each covering one point. So we need at least three sunny lines: one for (n,1), one for (n-1,1), one for (n-1,2). So k=2 impossible. This argument does not rely on having a vertical line for column n-2; it's independent. So the solution's claim that column n-2 forces a vertical line is not necessary; the impossibility of k=2 can be shown by focusing on columns n-1 and n. However, the solution's argument uses column n-2 to force a vertical line, then uses column n-1 and n to derive contradiction. The reasoning is valid: column n-2 has 3 points, so we need a vertical line (or three non-vertical lines). But the key contradiction is about covering (n,1) and column n-1 points with 